      {
        "slide": 6,
        "role": "evidence",
        "subject": "A row of three identical lilac glass lungs on a violet sweep under an orthographic three-quarter camera, each holding one gold bead. The first stands in a rounded frosted white enamel cradle. The second is crossed low by a bevelled periwinkle acrylic slab with engraved lines. The third is crossed high by a matching slab. Long plum-violet casts run back and right from all three.",
        "visual_consequence": "Without the headline, one of three is held in a different, white material and two of three are crossed by the same periwinkle scan plane, each at a different height. That reads as a share of one in three against two in three. The slabs sit at different heights, so the reader sees two different scans that both caught a bead. Equal lungs and one bead each keep it reading as a share rather than a count.",
        "weakness": "Minor. The lungs are simplified to tall bullet-shaped lobes. Anatomy rests on the ringed trachea and the frosted airway inside the glass, and it reads as lungs at feed size. Inside the glass bases there are swirling internal refraction highlights (tile r2c2, design x 540 to 900, y 650 to 800). These read as thick glass bottoms rather than a defect. The white cradle's meaning as a screening exam needs the dek. Its difference from the two periwinkle slabs reads without it.",
